                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>",
                    "text/markdown": "Based on the retrieved records, LLIS-RAGrets recommends reviewing material composition, dynamic performance under system-specific operating conditions, software/data interfaces, and environmental limits when evaluating a replacement component. Even when specifications appear identical, subtle differences in internal materials, timing, or interface assumptions can introduce failures.\n\n### Key LLIS Lessons and Evidence\n\n* **Physical and Material Recertification:** Substitute materials generally require recertification prior to use [LLIS 455]. In a flight life support system, an uncertified vendor material substitution in relief valves resulted in electrolyte decomposition and leaks [LLIS 455]. In critical applications, physical properties testing should be performed to confirm that material substitutions are acceptable [LLIS 455].\n* **In-Depth Analysis of Inherited Parts and Performance Assumptions:** Adopting an inherited part or design does not eliminate the need for in-depth analysis [LLIS 296]. For example, a Remote Interface Unit IC race condition was benign in Voyager due to slower circuit response requirements, but caused severe command output glitches in Topex because Topex required faster response [LLIS 296]. Electronic parts must be evaluated against actual electrical performance requirements and operational risks rather than assuming prior success guarantees reliability [LLIS 296].\n* **Software and Interface Re-evaluation:** When existing software or analytical tools are reused alongside new components or interfaces, independent reviews and formal interface documentation are necessary [LLIS 2158]. A failure to document and reverify software interface units resulted in an incorrect mirror figure being fabricated [LLIS 2158].\n* **Delta Assessment for Environmental and Power Differences:** When applying previously qualified hardware, delta certifications or assessments are used to evaluate differences in power quality, electromagnetic compatibility (EMI/EMC), and operational environments outside the original experience base [LLIS 1207]. \n\n### Case-Based Considerations for Reverification\n\n* **Material Compatibility:** If the replacement part contains substitute internal materials or sub-components, physical properties and chemical compatibility testing should be conducted to prevent unexpected degradation [LLIS 455].\n* **Timing and Dynamic Response:** If your system operates with specific response speeds or transient tolerances, test the replacement part under actual system timing and loads; identical nominal specs may mask internal race conditions or subtle response differences [LLIS 296].\n* **Interface and Software Alignment:** If the component connects to analytical tools or software control elements, formally reverify data units, scaling, and interface protocols [LLIS 2158].\n* **Environmental/Power Boundaries:** If the operating environment, power input, or EMI profile differs from the candidate part's baseline qualification, perform targeted delta testing for those specific environmental domains [LLIS 1207]."
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>",
                    "text/markdown": "**Retrieved records — expand to verify evidence**"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.HTML object>",
                    "text/html": "<details><summary>[LLIS 455] Nonmetallic Materials Certification</summary><p><a href=\"https://llis.nasa.gov/lesson/455\" target=\"_blank\">Original record</a></p><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\">DRIVING EVENT\nA flight life support system battery leaked when a vendor substituted the certified material with a substitute in the manufacturing of the relief valves and the electrolyte decomposed it. The vendor certification was for the original material only. Substitute materials generally require recertification prior to use.\n\nLESSON\nVendor&#x27;s materials certification processes must specify recertification for substitute materials.\n\nRECOMMENDATION\nIn critical applications, physical properties testing must be accomplished to assure that materials substitutions are acceptable.</pre></details>"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.HTML object>",
                    "text/html": "<details><summary>[LLIS 2158] Reuse of Analysis Software</summary><p><a href=\"https://llis.nasa.gov/lesson/2158\" target=\"_blank\">Original record</a></p><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\">ABSTRACT\nWhen a project plans to reuse existing metrology software for a new application, a thorough independent review of the software and its new interfaces should be conducted.\n\nDRIVING EVENT\nDuring the manufacturing of an x-ray optic, the sub-contractor proposed to reuse existing metrology analytical software to evaluate the in-process mirror figure. Given the mission criticality and limited budget this approach appeared reasonable. Reuse of portions of the software resulted in new interfaces between the old software elements and the new elements to be developed for the specific optics being manufactured. Without the rigor of formal software development documentation, a units mismatch was programmed into the analysis resulting in the incorrect mirror figure being fabricated. This error was realized by the contractor prior to delivery of the optic however it resulted in an extensive late delivery and cost overrun.\n\nLESSON\nReuse of critical metrology software for optics fabrication requires thorough independent review and formal documentation.\n\nRECOMMENDATION\nUnderstand the critical analytical software to be used for fabrication/testing of high cost/long lead item hardware. Perform independent reviews of the software to be reused and document the software interfaces. For large programs, formal software development requirements should be levied on both the reused elements and new software elements, through the use of formal software development, integration, testing, and configuration management methodologies.</pre></details>"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.HTML object>",
                    "text/html": "<details><summary>[LLIS 296] Glitches on the Discrete Command Outputs of the Remote Interface Unit (RIU) due to Inherent Integrated Circuit Race Condition</summary><p><a href=\"https://llis.nasa.gov/lesson/296\" target=\"_blank\">Original record</a></p><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\">ABSTRACT\nRIU glitches that resulted from a known design problem were benign for Voyager, but posed a serious problem for Topex due to its requirement for faster circuit response. When the glitches occurred during Topex assembly level acceptance test, costly modifications were necessary. Electronic parts should be utilized only as per manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations. Fully consider new assumptions, performance requirements, and potential risks when adopting an inherited design or inherited test plans.\n\nDRIVING EVENT\nAt assembly level acceptance test, 90% of the Topex RIU&#x27;s used on the discrete command channels experienced malfunctions (glitches). This problem resulted from the decision to do strobing of the power VCC on/off line rather than using a designated strobe line. This type of unconventional strobing generated a voltage race problem, which translated into an unequal voltage propagation delay internal to the chip, thus causing chip malfunctioning. This design decision dates back to the Voyager project. The RIU glitch phenomena occurred in Voyager, but was considered benign due to slower circuit response required by Voyager. On Topex, resolving the glitch problem required A) screening and assigning of sixteen RIU&#x27;s to locations less affected by glitches, and B) inserting RC filters on the central unit harness connector to eliminate glitches in spacecraft commands from the central processor.\n\nThe described costly Topex modifications were necessary to reduce the risk associated with the misuse of this part.\n\nAdditional Keyword(s): Poseidon\n\nReference(s): PFR #C04068\n\nLESSON\n1. Misuse of electronic parts can cause unexpected chip output response with serious mission impact. RIU&#x27;s are frequently utilized in executing critical subsystem spacecraft function.\n\n2. Use of &quot;inherited&quot; designs does not preclude the need to perform in-depth analyses of all elements.\n\nRECOMMENDATION\n1. Electronic parts should be utilized only as per manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations.\n\n2. Cognizant design engineers must consider fully the difference between the current and prior assumptions, electrical performance required, and potential risks when adopting an inherited design.\n\n3. Extreme care should be taken when applying any inherited designs since they do not ensure reliability. Similar care should be exercised when implementing inherited test plans.\n\nEVIDENCE\nJPL has referenced this lesson learned as additional rationale and guidance supporting Paragraph 6.6.2 (&quot;Engineering Practices: Inheritance&quot;) in the Jet Propulsion Laboratory standard &quot;Flight Project Practices, Rev. 7,&quot; JPL DocID 58032, September 30, 2008.</pre></details>"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.HTML object>",
                    "text/html": "<details><summary>[LLIS 1207] Capitalizing on Proven Processes</summary><p><a href=\"https://llis.nasa.gov/lesson/1207\" target=\"_blank\">Original record</a></p><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\">DRIVING EVENT\nPrior to flight 1A/R and 2A the International Space Station Program (ISSP) and the Space Shuttle Program (SSP) was found to have two separate certification requirements that required the hardware providers to furnish a delta certification on operational hardware with flight heritage and a good pedigree of performance. It was found that this was causing additional work, expense, and schedule problems as we got closer to flight time. This problem was identified in an EA presentation to the program on 10/29/98 and a process for resolution was formulated in a letter from Director Safety, Reliability, Maintainability, and Quality Assurance (SRM&amp;QA) on 12-8-98 (REF NA-98-085). This letter formed a &quot;Common Hardware Assessment Panel&quot; now known as the &quot;Grandfather Board&quot; to define the process and provide the disposition for each hardware certification.\n\nRoot cause: The root cause of the problem was that two independent programs established certification requirements for their vehicles. Since the vehicles serve different purposes and function differently it should have been expected that different certification requirements would be developed. Even with these differences it was found that the differences were minor and could be overcome with proper analysis and compromise.\n\nLESSON\nTake advantage of similar work and processes performed on prior and existing programs whenever possible to minimize duplication and promote efficiencies in new programs\n\nRECOMMENDATION\nDevelop a JSC Generic Requirements document for space flight equipment. This standard shall be applied for Criticality 3 (CRIT 3) hardware only and will be jointly approved by the ISSP and SSP. In addition to the JSC Generic Requirements Document, the following &quot;common hardware&quot; certification policy will be used to certify previously flown hardware.\n\n1. Any CRIT 3 hardware certified for use that has flown on the Space Shuttle or Mir Space Station and will be used for similar function on the ISS will be acceptable for the ISSP.\n\n2. No additional documentation is required except for the following:\n\n1. Hardware that will use Space Station power (United States on-orbit segment and /or international partner elements) will have a power quality, and a conducted and radiated emissions certification.\n\n2. Battery-operated hardware will be assessed using existing certification data to assure that Electromagnetic Interference/Electromagnetic Compatibility are not issues with the ISSP.\n\n3. Hardware launched, used, or stowed in environments outside the experience base will be assessed against existing certification data to assure the difference is not a safety or mission assurance issue\n\nEVIDENCE\nSee Director Safety, Reliability, Maintainability, and Quality Assurance (SRM&amp;QA) on 12-8-98 (REF NA-98-085). It was found that the differences in requirements between the ISS and Shuttle programs and between ISS partners were minor and could be overcome with proper analysis and compromise.</pre></details>"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                },
                {
                  "output_type": "display_data",
                  "data": {
                    "text/plain": "<IPython.core.display.HTML object>",
                    "text/html": "<details><summary>[LLIS 1220] Verification of program requirements at the element level</summary><p><a href=\"https://llis.nasa.gov/lesson/1220\" target=\"_blank\">Original record</a></p><pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap\">DRIVING EVENT\nThroughout 1A/R and 2A verification closure (stage level), considerable resources were expended gathering element level compliance data to provide closure of the identical requirements at the system level (ISS, USOS, AIRD).\n\nRoot cause: Initial planning did not take into account that some system level requirements are met if and only if the same requirements are met by each element on a given flight. Efficiencies can be realized by transferring the verification responsibility for such requirements to the Functional Configuration Audit (FCA) process.\n\nLESSON\nProper planning and allocation of verification roles and responsibilities must occur to ensure efficient, crisp verification of program requirements\n\nRECOMMENDATION\nIn staged or incremental verification planning, identify system level requirements that are met if and only if the corresponding requirements are met by the element on that flight. Use the VLN (verification logic network) to reflect that the verification of such requirements is taken care of by the elements&#x27; FCA process. No further closure is required at the system level. (This process is being implemented now among EV&amp;CS and other teams.)</pre></details>"
                  },
                  "metadata": {}
                }
              ]
            }
          },
